WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.900
(upbeat music)

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.960
- [Narrator] We mustn't be
victims but protagonists

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:04.280
of our stories.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:07.180
- I heard that you stalked
Steve McQueen for this role,

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:08.197
basically like...

00:00:08.197 --> 00:00:10.369
- I did not stalk him.

00:00:10.369 --> 00:00:13.020
- I'm kidding am kidding but
you did passionately want

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.580
to get this role the minute
you heard it announced?

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.850
- Yeah I get I didn't
know specifically what

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.700
the role would be but I just
knew that he had something

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.403
to do with bringing
Caribbean culture to the BBC.

00:00:24.403 --> 00:00:27.340
I was like I wanna be involved in that,

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:28.710
I don't care if I'm a tree,

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.640
I don't care if I'm the
person going to run off

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.920
to get the curry spice,

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:33.850
I wanna be involved.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:38.780
So that was in 2015 and yeah, here we are.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.660
- I mean, it's really incredible
both like you and John

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.890
coming to it because I
know you guys both did

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.470
the same preparatory acting program.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:50.560
And now really tell these
black British stories

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.410
on such a global audience.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.210
And it's something that's
universal over here in America.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:56.720
Our stories don't get told.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.870
So talk about what it's like to bring

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:00.560
a story that maybe people know about,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.060
but they don't know in detail.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.850
- Man, again, you'd only know about it

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:09.720
if you were a part of that
community and (indistinct)

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.000
at that time.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.970
It's not on a global scale

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:16.290
but we're pushing it out there
right now which is so great.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.470
It's great to be able
to have this opportunity

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:21.710
to be represented, your
culture being represented

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:24.190
within mainstream media.

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:27.570
Steve just being bold
and just being man enough

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.880
to stand by his word and
say this isn't his heart,

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.010
this isn't his heart 15 years ago.

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:35.750
And to see it through right
to the end is beautiful.

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:38.680
So it's a treat to come
to work and see people

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:39.513
that you connect with.

00:01:39.513 --> 00:01:41.980
I had shared the same culture as you,

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:43.680
it's just a beautiful tree

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.530
and just proud that this
is now being broadcasted

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:47.363
to the world.

00:01:47.363 --> 00:01:49.760
- What's really incredible too,
is it's not just one story,

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.860
it's several chapters and
it really gives an in-depth

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:58.010
sort of narration of
this particular community

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:00.720
throughout the time period
that they were not seen.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.790
But in doing so, you're
playing real life people,

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:07.390
and that's daunting as
well as I would assume,

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:11.110
very invigorating but what
was it like playing homage,

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.340
the real life woman that you're portraying

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.920
and how difficult was it to balance?

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.820
- Again, that was like, you're
constantly thinking about

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.080
the script and thinking about
the words that you have to say

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.560
and the scenes that you have to do,

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:26.850
and constantly checking
in with Steve to be like,

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:30.310
are you sure I feel it
says something like this?

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:31.700
Because you want her to be proud,

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:35.880
you want her to feel like you
correctly represented her.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.570
'Cause this story is
now taking the forefront

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:41.560
to be showcased.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:42.830
So you carry that.

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:44.700
It's a little bit of
nerves when you carry that.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:49.700
Is great because you're not
just having one story being told

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:51.200
and pushed to the forefront.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.550
You get into several different stories

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:54.950
that you didn't know about.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:59.040
So it's just something
fresh and something new,

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.390
for us right now in 2020
but these are stories

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:04.100
that were buried and hidden for years

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:08.180
So it's important its
important that we share it.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:11.200
- [Narrator] These are
new types of human beings.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.450
- Of the entire sort of
small acts anthology.

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:17.560
Yours is the only one that's based off

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:21.090
of like a completely fictional
story and dance and music

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:22.800
is just such a huge part of it.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.710
I was just curious, was that
daunting for you guys as

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.610
the cast to know you guys got to pull off

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:29.850
these amazing dance numbers?

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:32.340
- Definitely, at first I think
it was more the fact that

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.360
it wasn't based on a true
story that we were quite like,

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:35.540
Oh gosh, you know.

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:38.040
But afterwards, when we understood it

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:41.910
and we understood that this
episode is an escape from

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:45.020
the frustrations from
watching the other films,

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:47.100
cause I myself haven't had
the chance to see them yet,

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.930
but I know what they are about.

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:52.710
And, you know I can imagine
they're very hard hitting.

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:55.700
So it's almost as if our episode
is a house party in itself

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:57.850
of being able to escape.

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:00.590
Yeah, I also think it's still
even though it's not based

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:03.210
on a true story, there
is still people's truths.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:06.870
I spoke a lot to my mom and
my dad and these are that

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.750
it's still relatable it's still the truth.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.190
And there's gonna be so
many different moments

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:12.940
that people go, Oh my gosh, Oh yeah.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:15.270
I know my dad watching it
and something as small as

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:17.480
the guy is banging against
the wall in the party

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.990
and my dad was like, yes, yes, yes.

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:23.060
So it will still definitely relate.

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:25.680
- Yeah, and big ups to Steve

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.760
and the entire like production team

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:30.530
because the one thing I
know is I wasn't living

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:33.690
in the seventies but I
live for that time period.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.270
And although it was a
huge sixties and seventies

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:37.330
huge point of unrest,

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:40.690
it also provided some of
the most incredible music

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:43.140
and dance and Steve lived in that.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:46.570
So talk about him really
sort of immersing you guys

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:47.440
in this world.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.690
And then also how it was
just working with him

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:52.670
'cause I know he's a director
that gets great performances

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:53.910
from his actors.

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:56.720
- Oh he is incredible, definitely,

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:59.190
so Owen is the early eighties

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:03.870
and with Steve, he's so open
and trusting of his actors

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:06.400
to bring what they have to the table.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:09.580
We also worked with an incredible
choreographer called Carol

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:12.820
and her thing was she got
the supporting artists.

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:14.670
For example, before we started filming,

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:16.080
she got them in a room,
she said it's not enough

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.040
to just have them in costume.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.450
I need to play some music,
I need them to understand

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:22.060
this energy that needs
to be had for this film,

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:23.110
they need to know it.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:27.650
So I think even just
doing that, it was like,

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:30.410
there was no to me, there
was, we were all together.

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:31.690
It's not about leads or support

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:34.570
and everyone was just together
and as a whole in that.

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:38.100
And then she got us proper
on the dance in to understand

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.880
how different the dancing was back then.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:42.960
And to understand that
it's heavy in your feet

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.560
and it's grounded and you're
connected to the earth.

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:49.680
And I think that is obviously
why Steve asked Carol

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:51.800
to come and step in
because he really wanted us

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:55.060
to understand it's not just
about just dancing to dance.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.770
It means so much more it's
again people releasing

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:59.930
their frustrations, letting things out,

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:01.540
feeling it in their body.

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:04.140
It was so beautiful to be
able to experience that.

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:06.080
- Yeah, and like you said,
yours is the only one

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:10.400
that's not based off of history,
but it still hits within

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:13.440
the whole vibe of the
anthology over several decades.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:17.430
This idea of beauty
and art in the midst of

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:19.330
social and political unrest.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:22.150
And although yours leans
more in the entertainment

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:24.140
and the things that people did to escape,

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:27.000
all of the other stuff
is like so still there

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:28.120
and a part of it.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:29.620
And I'm actually curious though,

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:32.190
since it is more centered
in, on the dance stuff,

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:35.230
if you have a particular
moment from a dance movie,

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:36.677
besides yours

00:06:36.677 --> 00:06:37.980
(laughs loudly)

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:40.800
say like, 'cause I always
think to like this she's all

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.210
that dance moment.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:44.820
Like that's just like a random
dance moment in a teen movie,

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:45.653
but I love it.

00:06:45.653 --> 00:06:46.850
I wonder if you have one of those

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:48.408
that you particularly love.

00:06:48.408 --> 00:06:51.290
- One would be the dance in Death Proof.

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:53.867
Have you watched Death
Proof by Quentin Tarantino?

00:06:53.867 --> 00:06:55.870
(yells loudly)
Her(indistinct)

00:06:57.190 --> 00:06:59.750
I love Tarantino and I love...

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:02.061
that dance has always stayed with me.

00:07:02.061 --> 00:07:03.217
- [Narrator] They are leaders,

00:07:03.217 --> 00:07:06.060
but are rooted deep among those they lead.

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:09.920
- Playing real people,
there's always like a legacy

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:12.520
and a lot of homage you have
to pay to these real life

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:13.560
historical figures.

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:15.770
But then also try to tell
a compelling stories.

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:16.660
So I'll start with you

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:18.330
and then Malachi if you can talk about

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:20.140
portraying these real life characters

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:22.010
and how you approached it.

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:25.170
- Well, fortunately, you
know, I went to drama college.

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:26.003
What does that mean?

00:07:26.003 --> 00:07:28.450
And I say, it's a weird place
to answer these question,

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:31.110
but I feel it's not 'cause
I did went to drama college.

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:31.943
So what does that mean?

00:07:31.943 --> 00:07:35.650
Well, we were taught a method
of being able to access

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:39.750
ways and means to explore
different energies

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:43.890
and psychological mathematics
when it comes to portraying,

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:46.346
whatever it is that is
portrayed in the mangrove,

00:07:46.346 --> 00:07:47.970
in Frank specifically.

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:50.380
So, what do we know?

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:52.270
We know that this is a historical thing.

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:55.360
We also know that this is
not a pain that is unique.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:57.810
Frank is not the first and last person

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:01.990
to essentially suffer abuse
as a result of the color

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:03.580
of his skin.

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:07.983
So we know that, it seems
to be an ancestral cry

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:14.970
or something, maybe not an ancestral cry,

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:17.710
but it's about that feeling is about,

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:20.170
so we know that daunting, yes,

00:08:20.170 --> 00:08:22.720
because you've gotta take it
seriously, but fortunately,

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:24.560
we were built for that we're actors.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:26.860
It's not and it's no biggie.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:28.390
It is a biggie.

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:30.960
It having to play these
roles but fortunately,

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:33.290
we went to these places and
we've studied life enough

00:08:33.290 --> 00:08:34.960
and we've studied human nature enough

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:39.960
to be able to, when someone's
like anger, pain, frustration,

00:08:41.290 --> 00:08:46.290
abuse is the result of certain factors go.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:50.570
Fortunately, we got a lot of that stuff

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:52.040
in the back pocket already, right?

00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:54.563
All of us, you, Malachi our parents.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:09:00.470
So that's not easy, but
that's a help useful.

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:06.010
And then what you have is
the idea that says, yeah,

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:09.410
but this is not now this is them.

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:13.520
And what does that mean?
It's the same shit, sorry,

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:18.500
you know but it is, I see it's the same...

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:20.403
Oh yeah it's that in it.

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:23.900
- Yeah, it's problems
throughout the diaspora.

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:26.260
And then Malachi, if you
can go ahead and talk about,

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:28.173
again, playing the real person too.

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:33.180
- Yeah, I mean for me, we
have some other characters

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:36.320
in the Mangrove Nine that didn't have

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:38.140
I guess I don't wanna use the word fame,

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:42.290
but Darcus Howe was
known, he had a TV show.

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:47.290
He was the involved politically,
he was a known face.

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:50.710
And so there was a lot
more documents on him.

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:53.380
There's a lot more videos on
him, where you can actually

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:55.410
see him speaking and expressing himself.

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:59.190
And so for that, there's one
thing playing a real person

00:09:59.190 --> 00:10:01.170
and there's another thing
playing a real person that is

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:02.600
known to a community already.

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:05.390
And so there was a weight behind it,

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:08.440
even though I knew very
little about Darcus.

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:11.170
At first, there was a way
to kind of get this right

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:14.210
as there would be for every
character, to be honest,

00:10:14.210 --> 00:10:18.780
but more so for him I guess,
because he, like I said,

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:22.933
my mom knows who he was, he
grew up watching him on TV.

00:10:25.232 --> 00:10:28.000
And then you have to
remember as Shaun said

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:31.890
that you're an artist, so
he is a person that existed,

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:33.830
but so should every
character that you play,

00:10:33.830 --> 00:10:36.160
you should approach them
in the same way of bringing

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:38.170
the truth and light to this person

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:41.280
that was previously unknown
or at least unknown in part

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:44.030
and telling their story.

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:46.006
So it was approached in the same way.

00:10:46.006 --> 00:10:49.006
(slow upbeat music)

